<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">

<head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0" />
    <meta name="theme-color" content="#FFFFFF" />
    <base href="/" />
    @* No static <title> here: every page supplies its own through <PageTitle>, and with
       prerendering the HeadOutlet emits a real <title> element into this head. *@
    <meta name="description" content="bit Balette (Bit.Balette) - a Blazor-native, accessible, keyboard-first command palette: fuzzy search, nested pages, prefix modes, async sources, recents and frecency, global shortcuts and sequences, multi-select, prompts, theming and templates, with every decision made in C#." />

    @* Link previews: the card a chat client or a social site unfurls is the first impression a
       shared link makes. *@
    <meta property="og:type" content="website" />
    <meta property="og:site_name" content="Bit.Balette" />
    <meta property="og:title" content="Balette - a command palette for Blazor" />
    <meta property="og:description" content="The Ctrl+K box for Blazor apps: fuzzy search, nested pages, modes, async sources and global shortcuts - with matching, ranking and every decision in C#." />
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary" />

    <ResourcePreloader />
    @* The site kit's stylesheet (app.css - shared by every Utilities demo), then the library's.
       Bit.Balette does not add its stylesheet by itself: the host page links it, before any rule
       that restyles the palette through its custom properties. demo.css is that rule here - it
       styles this demo's samples and points the palette's tokens at the site's theme - so it comes
       after it. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["app.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["_content/Bit.Balette/bit-balette.css"]" />
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["demo.css"]" />
    @* Scoped-CSS bundle: includes the client project's *.razor.css files as well. *@
    <link rel="stylesheet" href="@Assets["Bit.Balette.Demo.Server.styles.css"]" />
    @* Synchronous on purpose: applies the light/dark theme before first paint. *@
    <script src="@Assets["theme.js"]"></script>
    <ImportMap />
    <link rel="icon" type="image/svg+xml" href="favicon.svg" />
    <HeadOutlet @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
</head>

@* data-enhance-nav="false": a link followed before the WebAssembly runtime has started is a plain
   page load rather than an enhanced one. Enhanced navigation fetches and merges the next page while
   the runtime is still starting, and the router the runtime then attaches can render the address
   it read before the merge - the URL says /getting-started and the page is the landing page. Once the
   app is interactive its router handles every link itself and this attribute is not consulted. *@
<body data-enhance-nav="false">
    @* InteractiveWebAssembly rather than Auto or Server: the palette matches and ranks on every keystroke,
       and in the browser that never waits on a network round trip. The pages are still prerendered, so the
       first response carries them. *@
    <Routes @rendermode="InteractiveWebAssembly" />
    <div id="blazor-error-ui" data-nosnippet>
        An unhandled error has occurred.
        <a href="." class="reload">Reload</a>
        <span class="dismiss">&#128473;</span>
    </div>
    <script src="@Assets["_framework/blazor.web.js"]"></script>
    <script src="@Assets["site.js"]"></script>
    @* The demo's own glue between the site kit and the palette: the site theme as the Theme command knows it,
       and which of the two palettes on this site a key belongs to. Its key listener is on the window, so it
       hears a key before the library's, which is on the document. *@
    <script src="@Assets["demo.js"]"></script>
</body>

</html>
